21-## 2026-11-04 (-28) — mirrored ahead of the money stream's three patches
22-
23-- **-28 is mirrored now, ahead of -23, -25 and -26 in the board's queue (Ryan, 2026-11-04: "yes, mirror it now").** All three reviewers said ready on r2. The money patches were not ready to mirror: -23 is in round 9, and -25 and -26 are unreviewed. *Why:* none of the three redefines an object -28 changes (checked: the only shared mention is -23's list of rate-version triggers, which -28 does not touch), and CI had already passed the four together. Mirroring lets the four CIs be re-graded and lets -29 build on a settled base, instead of -28 waiting days or weeks behind patches still in review. *Why not keep the queue order:* the queue serialises mirrors that could conflict; these do not, and the board's rule already makes each later patch re-run its battery on the current `tally` before its own mirror. *Cost:* the money patches now apply over a `tu.sql` that carries -28 (they strict-apply cleanly on the new build). *Where it lives:* `sql/DEPLOY-VERIFICATION.md` (v5.4.2-28), the board's mirror queue note.
24-
25-## 2026-11-04 (-28 r2) — the fee's state-agency check keeps reading the bill's time zone; every Texas county is loaded once as shared data
26-
27:- **Keep the check as built (option 1): the pipeline safety fee's state-agency exemption reads the bill's time zone for every non-zero fee under an exempting rule; load all 254 Texas counties and their zones once, as platform data, in a patch of their own (-29) (Ryan, 2026-11-04).** Round 1 of -28 (Opus S3) found that only El Paso and Hudspeth have a zone loaded, so a fee anywhere else in Texas is refused (known limit L1), and proposed reading the zone only when the agency answer differs between the earliest and latest possible midnight. Ryan: counties and their zones almost never change; it is data the database should hold once for every customer. *Why:* `places` and `place_facts` are already shared by every utility and written only by reviewed platform migrations (-16), so the cost the alternative avoided was a one-time load we owe anyway, not onboarding work per utility; with every county present the simple check never refuses for missing data, and a missing zone can never hide until a rare bill. *Why not the two-midnight shortcut:* extra logic whose only purpose was to avoid that load. *What stays per utility:* each premise's county membership — address data the utility records, needed also for city-limits sales tax (Kyle 2026-11-03 Q4); a premise with none is still refused. *Why its own patch:* -28 is mid-review; nothing is live, so -29 follows it. *Where it lives:* -28 r2 (L1 restated, no code change), -29 (Census county list; zones per 49 CFR 71.7).
28-
29-## 2026-11-02 (-28 r1) — percentage rates are stored as a percent to four decimals; entered and shown however the utility writes them
30-
31-- **A percentage rate is stored in percent, to four decimals; the app accepts it in any form the clerk types (percent or fraction), converts it, and shows it in the utility's preferred form (Ryan, 2026-11-02).** Round 1 of -28 (Opus S2) found the four-decimal check refuses Texas's gas gross receipts tax (Tax Code §182.022: 0.581%, 1.070%, 1.997%) and eighth-of-a-percent sales-tax pieces when they are stored as fractions (unit `decimal`: 0.01997 needs five decimals), while the same rate in percent (1.997) is accepted. *Why:* the rule is about storage; four decimals of a percent is exact for every statutory rate seen, and one stored form gives one meaning to "four decimals". A small utility's clerk who has always written fractions must not be refused or made to convert by hand: the app does the conversion. *Why not let the fraction unit carry six decimals in storage:* two stored forms of the same number, and a schedule override often takes its unit from the base item in another table, so the check would need a trigger. *Why not leave clerks to enter percent:* a refusal they cannot act on. *Where it lives:* APPLICATION-CONTRACTS AC-45; -28's four-decimal check is unchanged, and its refusal on a `decimal`-unit rate says to store percentages in percent. Retiring the `decimal` unit is a later rates patch.
32-
33-## 2026-11-02 (-28 r1) — a consolidated bill is fixed once it goes out: every member bill final at issue, nothing attaches after (money stream's rule)
34-
35-- **A summary bill is issued only when every member bill is final, and no member bill attaches to it after it is issued (Ryan, 2026-11-02; option 1).** Round 1 of -28 (Codex B2, Opus N1, Fable N3) issued a consolidated parent while one child was still a draft, then moved that draft to an El Paso premise and attached a second child in another zone; both were accepted, and the parent's pipeline-safety fee, checked at issue, was afterwards refused on recheck. The schema already freezes an issued bill's own address and parent (`enforce_invoice_immutable`, CI-012), but per bill: nothing stops a summary going out over draft members, or a member joining a sent summary. *Why:* in practice a child's premise never moves (a service address is fixed) and a summary covers a known set of addresses; the reviewers' sequence is data entry no office does on purpose. Freezing the family at issue matches how a billing clerk already works, and it also keeps a sent summary's total equal to the bills it adds up, which matters more than the fee's day boundary. *Why not recheck the parent whenever a child changes (option 2):* a trigger on every invoice write to defend a state that should not exist, and it would refuse the child's write without saying why the family is closed. *Why not accept it (option 3):* the fee is small, but the same gap lets a summary's total drift from its members. *Where it lives:* invoices are the money stream's; sent as a board note (HANDOFF.md, rates → money, 2026-11-02). -28 states the gap as a known limit that the money stream's rule closes.
36-
37-## 2026-11-02 (-23 r8) — AC-44's netting: per-state before an escheat report; recorded as an application to the shortfall (-27)
38-
39-- **-27 is drafted right after -23 mirrors, ahead of -24 (Ryan, 2026-11-02).** The money stream's order becomes -23 → -27 → -24, with -25 and -26 (drafted, not reviewed) reviewed in -27's waits; then enrollments, notices, new rule types as before. *Why:* -27's row is a draw on a credit and a repayment on a payment, through -23's position and cap logic, which is freshest now and was just reviewed by the same three reviewers. *Why not keep it last:* by then that logic is cold, and -27 would likely take more rounds. *Cost:* -24 (unmatched cash, agency pledges) slips by -27's length; nothing in -24 depends on -27. -27 is a patch the 10-29 schema estimate (about 15 after -23) did not count: roughly one more. *Where it lives:* the board's patch table; `handoffs/schema-money.md` at wrap-up; the roadmap at wrap-up.
40-- **Netting a credit against a bounced payment's shortfall is recorded as a new kind of application, pointed at the payment's shortfall, built as its own patch, v5.4.2-27 (Ryan, 2026-11-02; Opus r7 N3).** AC-44 called the netting "an ordinary application", but every application names an invoice, and the shortfall is on none: a bounce waits until the payment's bill applications are undone (those bills reopen and take a credit as usual), so what is left owed is money already paid out, chiefly a refund (Kyle's 10-21 example: 50 paid, 30 refunded, 50 bounced, 30 owed). *Why:* the row records that the credit's money paid the debt, so the credit reads 0 and its dormancy clock stops, the shortfall reads what is left, and the bank tie-out stays true; it also gives R24's never-closing credit a closing act. *Why not bill the shortfall as a charge, then apply to it:* Kyle (K-10, 10-21) rejected inventing a charge that never happened ("would look like a fee the customer was never assessed, which could cause trouble with the RRC and with customers"). *Why not record nothing (the core nets only in what it shows and allows):* the account balance is already right, but the credit keeps its money and its clock, the debt never reads paid, and the database still admits the credit's refund; a change in who holds the money is stored when it happens. *Why a follow-on patch, not round 8:* the new row is a draw on the credit and a repayment on the payment, through the position and cap logic that took -23 eight rounds; it is additive (no existing column changes meaning), so it costs no data migration later. *Where it lives:* APPLICATION-CONTRACTS AC-44; the board (-27 claimed, money stream).
41-- **Netting across payments before an escheat report is a per-jurisdiction rule the core reads, not a fixed step (Ryan, 2026-11-02; Opus r7 N3).** AC-44 had told the core to apply a credit to what the customer owes on another payment before refunding *or escheating* it. Before a refund that stands: it is Kyle's one-pot ruling (10-30) and the utility's own policy. Before an escheat report it is a setoff against a separate debt, and whether a holder may make one before reporting is each state's unclaimed-property law. *Why:* a Texas launch never justifies Texas-only design; statutory rules go in per-jurisdiction rows (rule-terms convention, 10-06). *Why not always net:* where a state bars the setoff the core would under-report, and fixing that later means reworking core logic built on it. *Why not never net:* where a state allows it, the utility remits money it could lawfully keep against a real debt. *Default where no rule row exists:* report the credit's own money in full and collect the debt separately (the conservative side). *Not checked:* no statute has been read yet; filling each state's row is the work this creates, and the schema has no per-state escheat rule table today (dormancy is hard-coded at three years in a view). *Within one payment:* not a setoff (the same money coming back); the database nets it everywhere. *Cost now:* contract wording only, no schema change. *Where it lives:* APPLICATION-CONTRACTS AC-44.
